@host = http://localhost:5160

### Prefer clicking? The Scalar UI is at {{host}}/scalar (and / redirects there).
### Every content endpoint there has an Accept-Language dropdown listing the active languages.

### This sample runs in BASE-LANGUAGE mode with BaseLanguage = "en" (see Program.cs):
###   * English lives in Products.Name / Categories.Name — real columns, visible in any SQL client
###   * Arabic and French live in Translations
###   * a missing translation falls back to the English column, so content is never null
###   * a product or category can only be CREATED with Accept-Language: en
###   * English cannot be deleted or deactivated

### ------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1. Languages — seeded with en (default), ar, fr
### ------------------------------------------------------------------

GET {{host}}/api/languages

### Add a fourth language (direction omitted -> inferred as "ltr")

POST {{host}}/api/languages
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "code": "es",
  "name": "Espanol",
  "isActive": true
}

### Hebrew: direction omitted -> inferred as "rtl" from the culture data

POST {{host}}/api/languages
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "code": "he",
  "name": "עברית"
}

### Direction can also be set explicitly ("ltr" or "rtl")

POST {{host}}/api/languages
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "code": "fa",
  "name": "فارسی",
  "direction": "rtl"
}

### Invalid code -> 400

POST {{host}}/api/languages
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "code": "not-a-code",
  "name": "Nope"
}

### Languages are addressed by code — the code is the identity and never changes

GET {{host}}/api/languages/ar

### Rename a language (full replacement of name, direction and active flag)

PUT {{host}}/api/languages/es
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Español",
  "isActive": true
}

### Promote Arabic to default (demotes English atomically)

POST {{host}}/api/languages/ar/set-default

### ...and back again

POST {{host}}/api/languages/en/set-default

### Deleting English -> 409. It is the base language: every entity's own table holds it and
### every other language falls back to it. Still refused after ar becomes the default above.

DELETE {{host}}/api/languages/en

### Deactivating the base language -> 409, for the same reason

PUT {{host}}/api/languages/en
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "English",
  "isActive": false
}

### Deleting a language that still has translations -> 409 with a hint

DELETE {{host}}/api/languages/fr

### Purge one language's translations; after this, DELETE {{host}}/api/languages/fr succeeds.
### (Skip this one if you want to follow the French examples below.)

DELETE {{host}}/api/languages/fr/translations

### ------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2. Read path — the same product in three languages
### ------------------------------------------------------------------

### Default language (no Accept-Language)
GET {{host}}/api/products

### Explicit language
GET {{host}}/api/products
Accept-Language: ar

### Quality-weighted negotiation: fr-FR has no exact match, so the bare "fr" is used
GET {{host}}/api/products
Accept-Language: fr-FR, fr;q=0.9, en;q=0.5

### Highest quality wins, regardless of order
GET {{host}}/api/products
Accept-Language: ar;q=0.4, en;q=0.8

### NOTE-A5 has no translation rows at all — every language reads its English columns
GET {{host}}/api/products/3
Accept-Language: ar

### MOUSE-01 has no French translation -> falls back to the English column
GET {{host}}/api/products/2
Accept-Language: fr

### Unknown / inactive language -> falls back to the default,
### and the Content-Language response header tells you what you got
GET {{host}}/api/products/1
Accept-Language: de

### What language did the API actually resolve?
GET {{host}}/api/current-language
Accept-Language: ar

### An unknown language is reported as not honoured
GET {{host}}/api/current-language
Accept-Language: de

### ------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3. Write path — one language per request (Accept-Language)
### ------------------------------------------------------------------

### Creating in a language other than the base one -> 409. The base value is what every
### translation hangs off, so it has to exist first.
POST {{host}}/api/products
Accept-Language: ar
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "sku": "HEADSET-AR",
  "price": 89.99,
  "name": "سماعة رأس لاسلكية"
}

### Create a product; Name/Description are stored as English, in the Products row itself
POST {{host}}/api/products
Accept-Language: en
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "sku": "HEADSET-02",
  "price": 89.99,
  "categoryId": 1,
  "name": "Wireless Headset",
  "description": "Over-ear headset with 30-hour battery life."
}

### Add Arabic to the same product — this one becomes a row in Translations, and the English
### column is left exactly as it was
PUT {{host}}/api/products/4
Accept-Language: ar
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "سماعة رأس لاسلكية",
  "description": "سماعة رأس بعمر بطارية 30 ساعة."
}

### Add French
PUT {{host}}/api/products/4
Accept-Language: fr
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Casque sans fil",
  "description": "Casque circum-auriculaire, 30 heures d'autonomie."
}

### Verify: every stored language for that product
GET {{host}}/api/products/4/translations

### Verify: each language reads back correctly
GET {{host}}/api/products/4
Accept-Language: ar

###
GET {{host}}/api/products/4
Accept-Language: fr

### Partial update: change only the price. Name/Description are omitted, so the English columns
### and every stored translation are left untouched — including under Accept-Language: ar, where
### the English value loaded from the column is never filed as an Arabic translation.
PUT {{host}}/api/products/4
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "price": 79.99
}

### Confirm the translations survived a price-only update
GET {{host}}/api/products/4/translations

### Update just the French name, leaving English and Arabic alone
PUT {{host}}/api/products/4
Accept-Language: fr
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Casque Bluetooth"
}

###
GET {{host}}/api/products/4/translations

### Unknown field -> 400 (request bodies reject anything the DTO does not declare)
PUT {{host}}/api/products/4
Accept-Language: fr
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "translations": { "ar": { "Name": "x" } }
}

### Remove one language's translation. A PUT cannot: a null field there means "not supplied".
### French reads of product 4 now fall back to the English columns; Arabic is untouched.
DELETE {{host}}/api/products/4/translations/fr

###
GET {{host}}/api/products/4
Accept-Language: fr

### English is the base language — the Products row itself, not a translation -> 409
DELETE {{host}}/api/products/4/translations/en

### Delete: the product's translation rows go with it, same transaction
DELETE {{host}}/api/products/4

###
GET {{host}}/api/products/4/translations

### ------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4. Class-level attribute — Category needs no per-property annotation
### ------------------------------------------------------------------

GET {{host}}/api/categories
Accept-Language: ar

### "books" has no French translation -> English fallback
GET {{host}}/api/categories
Accept-Language: fr

###
POST {{host}}/api/categories
Accept-Language: en
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "slug": "home-garden",
  "displayOrder": 3,
  "name": "Home & Garden",
  "description": "Everything for the house."
}

### Add Arabic to the new category
PUT {{host}}/api/categories/3
Accept-Language: ar
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "المنزل والحديقة",
  "description": "كل ما يخص المنزل."
}

### Slug is [NotMultiLingual], so it is identical in every language
GET {{host}}/api/categories/3
Accept-Language: ar

###
GET {{host}}/api/categories/3/translations

### ------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5. Search, sort and projections — on the TRANSLATED text, in the database
### ------------------------------------------------------------------
### A plain LINQ Where/OrderBy/Select on Name would only ever see the English column in this
### sample. WhereTranslation, OrderByTranslation and SelectTranslated use the requested language,
### falling back exactly as a read does.

### Search in French: matches "Ordinateur portable 14 pouces"
GET {{host}}/api/products?search=portable
Accept-Language: fr

### MOUSE-01 has no French name, so a French read shows the English one — and search matches it
GET {{host}}/api/products?search=wireless
Accept-Language: fr

### ...while a French search for "laptop" finds nothing: the French laptop is not called that
GET {{host}}/api/products?search=laptop
Accept-Language: fr

### Sort by the translated name (also sort=-name)
GET {{host}}/api/products?sort=name
Accept-Language: fr

###
GET {{host}}/api/products?sort=-name
Accept-Language: ar

### Unknown sort -> 400
GET {{host}}/api/products?sort=price

### A DTO projection with the category's name, both translated, in one query (SelectTranslated).
### "books" has no French name, so NOTE-A5's category falls back to "Books".
GET {{host}}/api/products/summaries
Accept-Language: fr

###
GET {{host}}/api/products/summaries
Accept-Language: ar

### ------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6. Localized error messages — Resources/en.json and Resources/ar.json
### ------------------------------------------------------------------
### Errors come back in the request's language, with the same fallback content has: there is no
### fr.json, so French callers get English. The library's own problems are translated in the same files.

### 404 in Arabic -> "المنتج 9999 غير موجود."
GET {{host}}/api/products/9999
Accept-Language: ar

### Same request in French -> no fr.json, so "Product 9999 was not found."
GET {{host}}/api/products/9999
Accept-Language: fr

### A library problem, translated under MultiLingual.Problems in ar.json -> "اللغة 'xx' غير موجودة."
GET {{host}}/api/languages/xx
Accept-Language: ar

### ------------------------------------------------------------------
### 7. Bulk deletes and orphaned translations
### ------------------------------------------------------------------
### ExecuteDelete bypasses SaveChanges, so the interceptor never removes the deleted products'
### translations. The endpoint purges them straight after: PurgeOrphanedTranslationsAsync deletes
### every translation row whose entity is gone. The response counts both.

### Delete every product in "electronics" (category 1): LAPTOP-14 and MOUSE-01, whose Arabic and
### French rows are then purged -> { "deletedProducts": 2, "purgedTranslations": 6 }
### (Run it last: the examples above use these products.)
DELETE {{host}}/api/products?categoryId=1

### categoryId is required -> 400
DELETE {{host}}/api/products
